<p>Federal prosecutors in Washington said in a new court filing that Cole Tomas Allen, 31, took a mirror selfie in his Washington Hilton hotel room at about 8:03 p.m. on April 25, minutes before he tried to enter the White House Correspondents’ Association dinner, where President Trump and hundreds of journalists were gathered. Prosecutors said an enhanced version of the image showed Allen wearing black clothing and a red tie and carrying what appeared to be an ammunition bag, a shoulder gun holster and a sheathed knife. Authorities said Allen was captured after trying to race past a security barricade near the ballroom, prompting an exchange of gunfire with Secret Service agents assigned to protect the event. President Trump was not hurt and was rushed off the stage; a Secret Service officer wearing a bullet-resistant vest was shot in the vest and survived. Allen’s federal defender said he is presumed innocent, while prosecutors asked a judge to keep him detained pending trial.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/XwV9wPDKKYb1yjCofFThzlM94RGYHKQu3gPhZdORBTFNLW5JUC0-NLEWWFM39q-LLdQM6iYlMO7FDl94iTtMDLTBQj_Uprj1sBNX9TyvYTO0cQRxer6MCAlHFCDUSLwcuSfsdrpPMBZaGGkGpndfHpDjiVP0ezU8zEAyWIEHmvnuBsIYF-YRxL98kuRT9go5Hv0gt6Y3zNEHDgnXg923khZkYUwsAuSB105iaeQO9x4PtIvZG2r9mLWa0rOQicFqcEhO0cGa_3Xy-772Og' alt='This image contained in a court filing by the Department of Justice, April 29, 2026, shows Cole Tomas Allen inside his hotel room on April 25 in Washington, using his cellphone to take a photograph of himself in the mirror.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Online checks: Prosecutors said Allen repeatedly checked websites tracking President Trump’s schedule, including live coverage of the president exiting his vehicle at the Hilton.</li><li>Scheduled emails: Investigators said preset emails with an “Apology and Explanation” attachment were sent at about 8:30 p.m., shortly before the attack began.</li><li>Hotel access: Prosecutors described Allen’s stay at the hotel where the dinner was being held as part of a plan to get around event security.</li><li>Criminal charges: Allen faces charges including attempted assassination, interstate transportation of a firearm with intent to commit a felony and discharge of a firearm during a crime of violence.</li><li>Political motive: U.S. Attorney Jeanine Pirro and three assistants signed a memorandum saying Allen sought to express political opinions through violence.</li></ul><blockquote>He intended to kill and fired his shotgun while trying to breach security and attack his target. Put simply, the defendant poses an uncommonly serious danger to the community if released pending trial. The defendant’s lack of criminal history and other personal circumstances do not alter this conclusion. - Federal prosecutors</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Federal prosecutors: Prosecutors said Allen was prepared for a mass-casualty event and argued that a successful attack would have caused “one of the darkest days in American history.” (<a href='https://sourcenm.com/2026/04/29/repub/suspect-charged-with-attempt-to-assassinate-trump-intended-mass-casualties-prosecutors-say/'>Source New Mexico</a>)</li><li>Neighbors: People interviewed about Allen’s background described his family as “really kind” and “wonderful people,” a contrast with prosecutors’ allegations. (<a href='https://www.stuff.co.nz/world-news/360972339/picture-perfect-beginnings-trumps-latest-would-be-assassin'>Stuff</a>)</li><li>President Trump: After returning to the White House, while still wearing his tuxedo, President Trump said officials believed the suspect acted alone. (<a href='https://www.wxyz.com/us-news/crime/man-charged-with-trying-to-kill-trump-at-dinner-took-photo-with-knife-in-hotel-investigators-say'>WXYZ</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/us-politics/article-man-charged-kill-trump-correspondents-dinner/'>Man charged with trying to kill Trump took photo with knife in hotel 